<sn><md>### Brief History

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20Fertility/MID%20Preparation/assets/eveolution-of-soil-fertilizer.jpeg)

- `Manures` and other materials have been applied to soils **for thousands of years** to increase plant growth.
- About **150 years ago**, some `essential plant nutrients` were discovered.
- Subsequently, `fertilizers containing essential plant nutrients` were manufactured and applied to soils to increase fertility.
- By **1855**, researchers at the `Rothamsted Agricultural Experiment Station`, near London, England, declared that `soil fertility could be maintained` permanently with mineral fertilizers.

</md></sn><sn><md>:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20Fertility/MID%20Preparation/assets/fertilizer-grade.jpeg)
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
### Fertilizer Grade

- An expression that indicates the `guaranteed minimum percentage of plant nutrients` in a mixed fertilizer containing two or three of the fertilizer elements.
- Order: N, P (expressed as P₂O₅), and K (expressed as K₂O) → **[N–P₂O₅–K₂O]**
- Example: Grade **27-3-9** contains:
  - 27% nitrogen
  - Phosphorus equivalent to 3% P₂O₅
  - Potassium equivalent to 9% K₂O
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### Fertilizer Ratio
- The `relative proportions of primary nutrients` in a fertilizer grade `divided by the highest denominator` for that grade.
- Example: A **27-3-9** fertilizer has a ratio of **9-1-3**.
  - High in nitrogen
  - Low in phosphorus
  - Moderate in potassium (relatively speaking)

</md></sn><sn><md>### Fertilizer Materials

- Fertilizers that contain `only one compound` and supply one or two plant nutrients are called **fertilizer materials** or **carriers**.
- The first fertilizer material was manufactured when `ground bones were treated with sulfuric acid` to increase the solubility (availability) of phosphorus.
  - Occurred in England around **1830**
  - Marked the beginning of the modern fertilizer industry
- This was followed by:
  - Development of **potassium materials** from dried sea deposits (late 19th century)
  - Manufacture of **nitrogen materials** from the `fixation of atmospheric N₂` (early 20th century)
</md></sn><sn><md>### Filler
- A substance added to fertilizer materials to:
  - Provide `bulk`
  - Prevent `caking`
  - Serve `some purpose` other than providing essential plant elements
- It makes up the difference between the weight of an added ingredient required and the total fertilizer that should be made according to the fertilizer formula.

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
Common filler materials:
  - Sand
  - Dolomitic limestone
  - Gypsum
  - Furfural
  - Vermiculite
  - Rock salt
  - Coal ashes
  - Chalk grit
  - Spent foundry sand
  - Peanut hulls
  - Saw dust
  - Rice husk
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20Fertility/MID%20Preparation/assets/common-filler-materials.jpeg)
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>Classes of Fertilizers

### Straight Fertilizer

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20Fertility/assets/straight-fertilizer.jpeg)

- Fertilizers containing `only one primary nutrient`.
- Also called **single fertilizer**.
- Examples:
  - Urea
  - Rock phosphate
  - Ammonia
  - Muriate of potash
- Occasionally, straight fertilizers may comprise a `mixture of ingredients` and may still supply only one nutrient.
  - Example: Some ++phosphatic fertilizers++ contain a mixture of `water-soluble` and `water-insoluble phosphate` but still supply only phosphorus.

</md></sn><sn><md>### Compound Fertilizers or Mixed Fertilizer

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20Fertility/assets/Compound-Fertilizer.jpeg)

- Fertilizers formulated with `two or more plant nutrients`.
- Defined as a `homogenous product` containing two or more of the following plant nutrients:
  - Nitrogen
  - Phosphorus
  - Potassium
  - Magnesium
- Forms: Granules, pellets, prills, or crystals.
- Characteristics:
  - Free flowing
  - Contain micronutrients
- Example: Compound phosphatic fertilizer
</md></sn><sn><md>### Complex Fertilizer

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20Fertility/assets/complex-fertilizer.jpeg)

- Contains **more than two plant nutrients**, of which `at least two major nutrients are in a chemical combination`.
- Each granule contains **all the nutrients** of the fertilizer.
- Generally granulated, free flowing, and can easily be applied uniformly.
- Examples:
  - Ammonium phosphates
  - Nitrophosphates
  - NPK complex
- Many crops need N, P, and K at the same time, so a wide range of complex fertilizers containing all three primary nutrients are produced for this purpose.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Organic Fertilizer

![organic-fertilizer](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20Fertility/assets/organic-fertilizer.jpeg)

- By-product from the processing of **animal or vegetable substances** that contain sufficient plant nutrients to be of value as fertilizers.
- Examples:
  - Composted cow manure
  - Cottonseed meal
  - Fish emulsion and fish meal
  - Hoof and horn meal
  - Kelp meal
  - Liquid seaweed

Advantages of Organic Fertilizer
- Slow release of available `water-insoluble` nitrogen and phosphate.
- Slow release minimizes the `leaching of nutrients`.
- Contain `no soluble salts` (like inorganic fertilizers) and can be applied in large quantities without damaging crops.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Biofertilizer
- A biofertilizer or `bioinoculant` is a substance which contains `living microorganisms` which, when applied to seeds, plant surfaces, or soil:
  - `Colonize the rhizosphere` or the interior of the plant
  - Promote growth by `increasing` the supply or availability of `primary nutrients` to the host plant

![img](https://binary.pensoft.net/fig/1159459/oo/file.jpg)

##### Types of Biofertilizers
Biofertilizers are mainly of **two types**:

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
#### a) Nitrogen Fixers
- Crops inoculated with nitrogen-fixing bioinoculant fix atmospheric nitrogen.
- Example: **Rhizobia** fix atmospheric nitrogen and make a symbiotic association with leguminous plants.
  - Rhizobia supply nitrogen to plants.
  - Receive photoassimilated carbon from the legume.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
#### b) Phosphate Solubilizers
- A few heterotrophic bacteria and fungi, also called **phosphate solubilizing microorganisms**, solubilize inorganic phosphorus.
- The symbiotic relationship of plant roots and fungi, like **arbuscular mycorrhizae** or **vesicular arbuscular mycorrhizae**, mobilize phosphate ions.
:::
:::

##### Advantages of Biofertilizers
- Slow-release character is their major advantage.
- Other benefits:
  - Plant nutrition
  - Disease resistance
  - Soil fertility
  - Tolerance to adverse soil or climatic conditions
- Help counter:
  - Increased soil salinity
  - Chemical run-off from agricultural fields
</md></sn><sn></sn>